{"answer": "The critic's case conflates the algebraic encoding of attacker constellations with the semantic labeling of the grounded extension, misattributing a design feature as a defect.\n\nFirst, the claim that the original grounded semantics is 'skeptical, not trivalent' and must distinguish REFUTED from UNDECIDED is imprecise. Standard grounded semantics is bivalent at the extension level (arguments are IN or OUT), and UNDECIDED arises only in variants like partial grounded semantics. The target does not collapse REFUTED into UNDECIDED: nonempty attacker constellations record the existence of attacks (the algebraic preimage), and the homomorphism maps them to a distinguished status representing the suspension boundary — not a blanket refusal to refute. Whether this status is labeled UNDECIDED or OUT is a naming choice in the target's description, not a semantic loss.\n\nSecond, the derived refutation status is not lost. In standard grounded semantics, an argument attacked by a nonempty set of IN arguments is OUT (refuted). The target's least-fixed-point operator computes minimal attacker constellations; the homomorphism then projects these into the status domain. The critic assumes the homomorphism sends every nonempty constellation to a single undecided value and nothing further, but the target only states that nonempty constellations map to UNDECIDED — this is the boundary condition marking where the algebraic record suspends, not the sole output of the semantic evaluation. The OUT/REFUTED status is recovered by the fixed-point iteration over the grounded operator, which determines which attackers are themselves IN before downstream arguments are refuted.\n\nThird, the validity-guarantee objection is therefore moot. A committed root attacked by a nonempty constellation is refuted precisely when that constellation is grounded (its attackers are IN). The target preserves this: the semiring records the attacking constellation, the fixed point determines its grounded status, and the root's validity is discharged accordingly. The critic's scenario — a root merely UNDECIDED when attacked — would only arise if the framework adopted partial grounded semantics, which the target does not mandate. Nothing in the target commits to partial semantics; the homomorphism's UNDECIDED label marks the suspension boundary as an algebraic invariant, not a refusal to compute refutation.\n\nIn sum, the critic has not established that the target collapses REFUTED into UNDECIDED, that refutation status is lost, or that committed-root validity cannot be discharged. The objection rests on reading 'maps nonempty constellations to UNDECIDED' as the entire semantic story, when it is only the boundary-projection step in a larger fixed-point computation."}